        <Sticky classid="Sticky" id="815627732" x="162" y="36" w="846" h="414" colors="0, 15792890">
          <paramlist>
            <param name="full_font" value="Tahoma, 10pt" />
            <param name="align" value="0" />
            <param name="fg_color_adv" value="#FF000000" />
            <param name="bg_color_adv" value="#FFFFFFFF" />
            <param name="hl_color_adv" value="#FFFFFF00" />
            <param name="bdr_color_adv" value="#FF808080" />
            <param name="arrows" value="0" />
            <param name="style" value="1" />
            <param name="font" value="1" />
            <param name="fg_color" value="0" />
            <param name="bg_color" value="15792890" />
          </paramlist><![CDATA[This case demonstrates how to use the modules in DC_EMT_lib.pslx to build a full-scale data center model for power system dynamic studies. The model uses average converter representations and includes disturbances on both the grid and workload sides. The simulation sequence is as follows:

1. Initialization (t = 0~5 s): The IT load is initialized to 0.6 pu, while the cooling load, represented by an induction motor, is brought to its speed setpoint.

2. Grid-side disturbance (t = 5 s): A voltage dip is applied at t = 5 s, and the voltage begins to recover at t = 6s. This event demonstrates the responses of the data center components, particularly the UPS, to the voltage dip and subsequent recovery according to its voltage ride-through logic.

3.Workload-side disturbance (t = 12 s onward): A periodic square-wave variation is applied to the IT workload to represent power demand fluctuations associated with AI training tasks.]]></Sticky>
